__d(function(e,t,i,o,a,n,r){a.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:10,chapter_title:"Motion and Measurement of Distances",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is motion? Give two examples.",back:"Motion is the change in position of an object with respect to its surroundings over time. An object is said to be in motion if its position changes relative to a fixed reference point. Examples: a moving bus changes its position on the road (motion); the hands of a clock change their position on the dial (motion). An object is at rest if its position does not change with time relative to a fixed point.",memory_hook:"MOTION = Movement Over Time In Our Neighbourhood. If your position changes, you are in motion. Freeze tag: frozen = rest, running = motion."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the SI unit of length? What does SI stand for?",back:"The SI unit of length is the metre (m). SI stands for Syst\xe8me Internationale d'Unit\xe9s \u2014 the International System of Units. It is the globally accepted standard system of measurement used in science. Other SI length units: kilometre (km = 1000 m), centimetre (cm = 1/100 m), millimetre (mm = 1/1000 m). The metre is defined as the distance light travels in vacuum in 1/299,792,458 of a second.",memory_hook:"SI = Scientists' International agreement. The METRE is their ruler. 1 km = 1000 m = 100,000 cm = 1,000,000 mm. Go big or go small \u2014 it's all metres."},{id:"fc03",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Convert: (a) 5 km to metres (b) 250 cm to metres (c) 3.5 m to cm",back:"(a) 5 km = 5 \xd7 1000 = 5000 m. (b) 250 cm = 250 \xf7 100 = 2.5 m. (c) 3.5 m = 3.5 \xd7 100 = 350 cm. Key conversions: 1 km = 1000 m; 1 m = 100 cm; 1 cm = 10 mm; 1 m = 1000 mm. Always multiply when converting to a smaller unit, divide when converting to a larger unit.",memory_hook:"Kilo = 1000, centi = 1/100, milli = 1/1000. Going smaller (km \u2192 m \u2192 cm \u2192 mm): MULTIPLY. Going bigger (mm \u2192 cm \u2192 m \u2192 km): DIVIDE."},{id:"fc04",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the three types of motion? Give one example of each.",back:"The three types of motion are: (1) Rectilinear motion (straight-line motion) \u2014 a car moving on a straight road, a ball thrown vertically upward; (2) Circular motion \u2014 a merry-go-round, hands of a clock, Earth revolving around the Sun; (3) Periodic motion \u2014 a swinging pendulum, a vibrating guitar string, the Earth rotating on its axis. Many objects show more than one type of motion simultaneously \u2014 a wheel rolling forward shows both circular and rectilinear motion.",memory_hook:"RCP: Rectilinear = Road, Circular = Carousel/Clock, Periodic = Pendulum. RCP! Remember: Road, Carousel, Pendulum."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is rectilinear motion? Give two examples from daily life.",back:"Rectilinear motion is motion along a straight line (also called linear motion). The object moves in one direction without curving. Examples: (1) a train moving on a straight railway track; (2) a ball rolling straight on a flat floor; (3) a person walking in a straight corridor; (4) a bullet fired from a gun (approximately). Most real-world motion is not perfectly rectilinear, but when the path is approximately straight, we treat it as rectilinear.",memory_hook:"RECTILINEAR = RECT (Latin for straight) + linear (line). Straight as a ruler. Trains on straight tracks = perfect rectilinear motion."},{id:"fc06",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is circular motion? Is a person sitting on a merry-go-round in circular motion?",back:"Circular motion is motion along a circular path \u2014 the object keeps changing direction as it moves in a circle. Yes, a person sitting on a merry-go-round is in circular motion \u2014 their position constantly changes as they move in a circular path around the centre. Other examples: a ceiling fan's blades, the Moon orbiting Earth, hands of a clock, a ball on a string being swung in circles, a planet orbiting the Sun.",memory_hook:"CIRCULAR = moves in a CIRCLE. Think of anything that goes round and round \u2014 fan, clock, carousel, Earth around Sun. All circular motion."},{id:"fc07",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is periodic motion? How is it different from circular motion?",back:"Periodic motion is motion that repeats itself after a regular interval of time. The object returns to the same position repeatedly. Examples: a swinging pendulum, a vibrating tuning fork, the Earth's rotation (one rotation every 24 hours), a bouncing ball (approximately). Difference from circular motion: circular motion is specifically along a circular path; periodic motion just needs to repeat \u2014 it can be circular (fan), back-and-forth (pendulum), or up-and-down (bouncing). All circular motion is periodic, but not all periodic motion is circular.",memory_hook:"PERIODIC = happens with a PERIOD (time interval). Like a period at the end of a sentence \u2014 it repeats. Pendulum: tick, tock, tick, tock \u2014 perfectly periodic."},{id:"fc08",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why was the development of standard units of measurement important?",back:"Before standard units, people used body parts (handspan, cubit \u2014 length of forearm) or arbitrary objects to measure. These were inconsistent because everyone's hand size and arm length differs. Problems: trade disputes (selling fabric by handspan gave different amounts to different buyers); construction errors (if different workers used different body measurements); scientific confusion (experiments couldn't be compared across countries). Standard units ensure everyone uses the same measurement everywhere, making trade fair, science reproducible, and engineering precise.",memory_hook:"The KING'S FOOT problem: if the unit of length was the king's foot, what happens when a new king with smaller feet is crowned? Everything changes. Standard units end this chaos."},{id:"fc09",type:"How/Why",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do you correctly use a ruler/metre scale to measure length?",back:"Correct method for measuring length with a ruler: (1) Place the ruler along the object to be measured; (2) Start from the zero mark (0 cm), not the edge of the ruler; (3) Ensure the ruler is flat against the object, not tilted; (4) Read the measurement from directly above the mark (not from an angle \u2014 parallax error); (5) Record the measurement with the correct unit (cm or mm). Common errors: starting from 1 cm instead of 0 cm (gives reading 1 cm too small); tilted ruler (gives longer reading); reading from angle (parallax error).",memory_hook:"START AT ZERO. Always. Reading a ruler from the edge instead of zero is the most common mistake. Zero = start, end mark = measurement."},{id:"fc10",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How would you measure the length of a curved line (like the length of a winding river on a map)?",back:"To measure a curved line: Method 1 \u2014 Thread method: place a thread along the curve, following all bends; mark the end point; then straighten the thread and measure its length with a ruler. Method 2 \u2014 Divider method: use a divider (compass) to step along the curve in small equal intervals, count the steps, then multiply by the interval length. Both methods convert a curved length into a straight-line measurement. The thread method is most practical for school use.",memory_hook:"Curved = Thread it! Lay a thread along the curve like a snake following a path. Straighten the snake, measure it. Curved becomes straight."},{id:"fc11",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What were three non-standard (traditional) units of length used before SI units?",back:"Traditional non-standard units: (1) Handspan \u2014 distance from tip of little finger to tip of thumb when the hand is fully spread (about 20 cm); (2) Cubit (Hasta) \u2014 distance from the elbow to the tip of the middle finger (about 45 cm); (3) Foot \u2014 the length of a person's foot (about 30 cm, now standardised to exactly 30.48 cm in Imperial system); (4) Yard \u2014 distance from nose to fingertip of outstretched arm; (5) Pace \u2014 the length of one step. In India, the gaz (yard equivalent) and angul (finger width) were used.",memory_hook:"Body = ancient ruler. Hands for short lengths, feet for medium, arms outstretched for longer. Humans were the original measuring instruments."},{id:"fc12",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A wheel rolls forward along a road. What types of motion does it show?",back:"A rolling wheel shows TWO types of motion simultaneously: (1) Circular motion \u2014 the wheel spins/rotates on its axle, following a circular path. Any point on the wheel moves in a circle; (2) Rectilinear motion \u2014 the wheel (and the vehicle it is part of) moves forward in a straight line along the road. This is a compound motion. Similarly, the blades of a ceiling fan show circular motion (spinning), and if the fan is moved from one room to another, it shows rectilinear motion too. Most real-world motions are compound.",memory_hook:"ROLLING = SPIN + TRAVEL. The wheel spins (circular) AND moves forward (rectilinear). Think of a ball rolling down a road \u2014 it's doing two dances at once."},{id:"fc13",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Is the Earth's motion around the Sun rectilinear, circular, or periodic?",back:"The Earth's motion around the Sun is: (1) Circular (approximately \u2014 actually elliptical, but treated as circular at school level) \u2014 Earth moves in a roughly circular orbit around the Sun; (2) Periodic \u2014 Earth completes one full orbit every 365.25 days (one year), repeating this journey endlessly. So it is BOTH circular and periodic. The Earth's rotation on its axis is also circular and periodic (one rotation every 24 hours = one day). Circular motion is always periodic because the object returns to its start position after each revolution.",memory_hook:"Earth = the ultimate CLOCK. It spins (circular, periodic \u2014 24 hours) AND orbits (circular, periodic \u2014 365 days). Two circular periodic motions at once."},{id:"fc14",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between rest and motion?",back:"An object is at REST when its position does not change with respect to a fixed reference point over time. An object is in MOTION when its position changes with respect to a fixed reference point over time. Important: rest and motion are relative \u2014 the same object can be at rest relative to one observer and in motion relative to another. Example: a passenger sitting in a moving train is at rest relative to another passenger in the same train, but in motion relative to a person standing on the platform.",memory_hook:"REST vs MOTION = depends on WHO IS WATCHING. The train passenger story: same person, same moment \u2014 at rest to one, in motion to another. Relativity of motion!"},{id:"fc15",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is it important to have a reference point when describing motion?",back:"A reference point (or reference frame) is a fixed point from which we measure the position of an object. Without a reference point, we cannot say whether something is moving or at rest. Example: Is a tree 'moving'? From Earth, no. From a spaceship observing Earth rotate, yes \u2014 the tree moves with Earth's rotation. We always need to specify 'moving relative to what.' In everyday life, we use the ground or our own position as the reference point. This concept is the basis of Einstein's theory of relativity.",memory_hook:"REFERENCE POINT = the 'HQ' of observation. Every motion needs a HQ. Without it, you can't tell if the bus is moving or the scenery is moving past you."},{id:"fc16",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the standard SI prefixes: kilo, centi, and milli? Give their values.",back:"SI prefixes: kilo (k) = 1000 times the base unit. 1 kilometre = 1000 metres. Centi (c) = 1/100 of the base unit. 1 centimetre = 1/100 of a metre. Milli (m) = 1/1000 of the base unit. 1 millimetre = 1/1000 of a metre. Summary: 1 km = 1000 m; 1 m = 100 cm; 1 m = 1000 mm; 1 cm = 10 mm. These prefixes are used with all SI units, not just length: kilogram, milligram, centilitre, etc.",memory_hook:"Kilo = King (1000 times bigger). Centi = Cent (1/100 \u2014 like 1 cent is 1/100 of a rupee). Milli = Mini (1/1000 \u2014 very tiny)."},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Classify each motion: (a) a child on a swing (b) a car on a highway (c) a spinning top (d) a bouncing ball",back:"(a) Child on a swing \u2014 Periodic motion (swings back and forth in a regular time interval); also approximately circular. (b) Car on a highway \u2014 Rectilinear motion (approximately straight-line motion). (c) Spinning top \u2014 Circular motion (rotates on its axis in a circular path); also periodic. (d) Bouncing ball \u2014 Periodic motion (bounces up and down in a regular rhythm). Note: many motions belong to more than one category. The key is identifying the dominant type for the context.",memory_hook:"Swing: back-and-forth = Periodic. Car on road = Rectilinear. Top spinning = Circular + Periodic. Bouncing ball = Periodic. Ask: straight? circular? repeating?"},{id:"fc18",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How has the development of transport changed the measurement of distances in India?",back:"As transport developed (from walking to bullock carts to trains to aircraft), the distances humans needed to measure and compare changed dramatically. Railway lines required precise km measurements. Road networks needed accurate maps. Air travel needed distances measured in thousands of km. Today, Indian Railways measures all distances in km; highway signs are in km; speed is in km/h. GPS and odometers now measure distances automatically. Karnataka's road network spans over 2.5 lakh km, all measured using standardised units derived from the metre.",memory_hook:"TRANSPORT = DISTANCE MEASURER. As we moved faster and farther, we needed better, standard units of distance. Trains forced India to adopt standard km measurements."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A student measures a book's length using her handspan and gets 2.5 handspans. Her friend measures the same book and gets 2 handspans. Why are the measurements different?",back:"The measurements are different because the handspan is a non-standard unit \u2014 it varies from person to person depending on the size of their hand. The student with a smaller handspan will get a larger number (more handspans to cover the same length); the student with a larger handspan will get a smaller number. This illustrates the problem with non-standard units: they are inconsistent and cannot be reliably communicated. Standard units (metres, centimetres) remove this problem because 1 cm is exactly the same for everyone, everywhere.",memory_hook:"Non-standard units = PERSONAL and UNRELIABLE. Your handspan \u2260 my handspan. That's why SI was invented \u2014 so everyone measures with the same 'handspan.'"},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"The Bengaluru Metro (Namma Metro) covers 73 km of track. Express this in (a) metres (b) centimetres.",back:"(a) 73 km = 73 \xd7 1000 = 73,000 metres (73 km = 73,000 m). (b) 73,000 m = 73,000 \xd7 100 = 7,300,000 cm (7.3 million centimetres). These conversions show why we use km for large distances and cm for small ones \u2014 expressing 73 km in cm gives an unwieldy number. The choice of unit should match the scale of measurement: cm for small objects, m for rooms and buildings, km for roads and cities.",memory_hook:"Namma Metro = 73 km. \xd7 1000 = metres. \xd7 100 again = cm. Always multiply when going to a smaller unit. The zero-adding machine: km \u2192 m (add 3 zeros), m \u2192 cm (add 2 zeros)."}]}},2905,[]);